From 6a8c51553cab05bb485f770890bf7b6d1f483c73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 08:14:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: refill the RX ring with page pool buffers without an skb

Every buffer of the refill ring gets an skb when it is posted: build_skb()
on the page pool fragment, its sk_buff from the slab and the clearing of
both. A TCP segment that then joins a run of the REO reap's merge gives
its buffer to the run's head and frees that skb again, unused. In a
profile of bridged uplink at 850 Mbit/s (IPQ8074, one run) build_skb(),
__build_skb_around(), __build_skb(), kmem_cache_alloc_noprof(),
___slab_alloc() and memset take about 5.5 % of all samples.

Post page pool buffers bare: the table entry of such a buffer is its
address, and the state an skb's control block held (the DMA address, the
synced length, what the REO entry says) is kept in the buffer where the
skb's shared info goes once one is built on it. The device writes no more
than DP_RX_BUFFER_SIZE bytes of a buffer, as the coalescing of an MSDU that
spans buffers already relies on, and the CPU syncs no more than the skb
data area in front of the shared info, so neither overwrites that state.
An skb a table holds anyway, the defragmented frame the driver reinjects,
is told apart by bit 0 of its entry.

The REO reap takes the buffers in their order without skbs. An MSDU in
one buffer then gets the checks of ath11k_dp_rx_process_msdu() and the
part of ath11k_dp_rx_h_mpdu() that only reads the frame (peer, station,
source address and type, the decryption and error status) on the buffer
itself, now a function of its own that the skb path calls too, and when
it is the next segment of an open run with fragments it joins as one,
with the same checks ath11k_dp_rx_merge_rx() makes of an skb; its page
pool reference becomes the head's fragment, as skb_try_coalesce() moves
it, and it never gets an skb. Every other MSDU gets its skb from the NAPI
cache at that point and takes the old path. An MSDU over several buffers
gets the skbs of all of them, and the error, exception and monitor paths
get theirs from the table lookup, which builds one for a buffer without
and puts it in the buffer's place.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
